'''trait''' (trāt, in Great Britain trā), ''n.'' [< OF. ''trait'', ''traict'', a line, stroke, feature, tract, etc., F. ''trait'', a line, stroke, point, feature, fact, act, etc., = Pr. ''trait'', ''trag'', ''trah'' = It. ''tratto'', a line, etc., < L. ''tractus'', a drawing, course: see ''tract''<sup>1</sup>, ''n.'', of which ''trait'' is a doublet. Cf. also ''trace''<sup>2</sup>, orig. ''trais'', pl. of OF. ''trait''.] 1. A stroke; a touch.

{{smaller block|By this single ''trait'', Homer makes an essential difference between the Iliad and Odyssey.
{{right|''W. Broome'', Notes on the Odyssey, i. 9.}}
From talk of war to ''traits'' of pleasantry.
{{right|''Tennyson'', Lancelot and Elaine.}}}}

2. A distinguishing or peculiar feature; a peculiarity: as, a ''trait'' of character.

{{smaller block|He had all the Puritanic ''traits'', both good and evil.
{{right|''Hawthorne'', Scarlet Letter, Int., p. 8.}}
One of the most remarkable ''traits'' in modern Egyptian superstition is the belief in written charms.
{{right|''E. W. Lane'', Modern Egyptians, I. 318.}}}}

'''traiterie†''', ''n.'' An old spelling of ''traitory''.

'''traitor''' (trā′tọr), ''n.'' and ''a.'' [Early mod. E. also ''traitour''; < ME. ''traitour'', ''traytour'', ''treitur'', < OF. ''traitor'', ''traitur'', ''traiteur'', ''traistre'', F. ''traître'' = Pr. ''trahire'', ''traire'', ''trahidor'', ''traidor'', ''traitor'' = Sp. Pg. ''traidor'' = It. ''traditore'', < L. ''traditor'', one who betrays, a betrayer, traitor, lit. ‘one who delivers,’ and hence in LL. also a teacher, < ''tradere'', give up, deliver: see ''tradition'', ''tray''<sup>3</sup>, and cf. ''traditor''.] '''I.''' ''n.'' 1. One who violates his allegiance and betrays his country; one who is guilty of treason. See ''treason''.

readings.c1s3:
¶
{{smaller block|God wole not that it be longe in the Hondes of ''Traytoures'' ne of Synneres, be thei Cristene or othere.
{{right|''Mandeville'', Travels, p. 74.}}}}

{{smaller block|Alle tho that ne wolde not come, he lete hem well wite that thei sholde haue as streyte Iustice as longed to theuis and ''traytoures''. {{right|''Merlin'' (E. E. T. S.), ii. 205.}}}}

{{smaller block|William's Fortune secures him as well at home against ''Traitors'' as in the Field against his Enemies.
{{right|''Baker'', Chronicles, p. 25.}}}}

{{smaller block|There is no difference, in point of morality, whether a man calls me ''traitor'' in one word, or says I am one hired to betray my religion and sell my country. {{right|''Swift.''}}}}

'''2.''' One who betrays any trust; a person guilty of perfidy or treachery; one who violates confidence reposed in him.

{{smaller block|If you flatter him, you are a great ''traitor'' to him.
{{right|''Bacon.''}}}}

='''Syn.''' 1. ''Rebel'', etc. See ''insurgent''.

'''II.''' ''a.'' Of or pertaining to a traitor; traitorous.

{{smaller block|And there is now this day no gretter treson thanne a gentille woman to yeue her selff to a ''traitour'' fals churle, blamed with vices, for there is mani of hem deceiued bi the foule and grete fals othes that the fals men vsen to swere to the women. {{right|''Knight of La Tour Landry'', p. 2.}}}}

{{smaller block|Their silent war of lilies and of roses,
Which Tarquin view'd in her fair face's field,
In their pure ranks his ''traitor'' eye encloses.
{{right|''Shak.'', Lucrece, l. 73.}}}}

'''traitor†''' (trā′tọr), ''v. t.'' [< ''traitor'', ''n.''] To act the traitor toward; betray.

{{smaller block|But time, it ''traitors'' me. {{right|''Lithgow.'' (''Imp. Dict.'')}}}}

'''traitoress''' (trā′tọr-es), ''n.'' [< ''traitor'' + ''-ess.''] A female traitor; a traitress.

{{smaller block|Fortune, . . .
The false ''trayteresse'' pervers.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Death of Blanche, l. 813.}}}}

readings.c2s1:
¶

'''traitorful†''' (trā′tọr-fu̇l), ''a.'' [ME. ''traitourfull''; < ''traitor'' + ''-ful.''] Traitorous; treacherous.

{{smaller block|My ''traitourfull'' torne [action] he turment my tene.
{{right|''York Plays'', p. 316.}}}}

'''traitorism''' (trā′tọr-izm), ''n.'' [< ''traitor'' + ''-ism.''] A betrayal. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|The loyal clergy . . . are charged with ''traitorism'' of their principles. ''Roger North'', Examen, p. 323. (''Davies.'')}}

'''traitorly†''' (trā′tọr-li), ''a.'' [< ''traitor'' + ''-ly''<sup>1</sup>.] Treacherous; perfidious.

{{smaller block|These ''traitorly'' rascals, whose miseries are to be smiled at, their offences being so capital. ''Shak.'', W. T., iv. 4. 821.}}

'''traitorous''' (trā′tọr-us), ''a.'' [Formerly also ''traiterous''; < ME. ''traitorous''; < ''traitor'' + ''-ous.''] 1. Guilty of treason; in general, treacherous; perfidious; faithless.

{{smaller block|More of his [majesty's] friends have lost their lives in this rebellion than of his ''traitorous'' subjects.
{{right|''Addison'', Freeholder, No. 31.}}}}

2. Consisting in treason; characterized by treason; implying breach of allegiance; perfidious: as, a ''traitorous'' scheme or conspiracy.

{{smaller block|''Vol.'' My name 's Volturtius,
I know Pomtinius.
''Pom.'' But he knows not you,
While you stand out upon these ''traitorous'' terms.
{{right|''B. Jonson'', Catiline, iv. 7.}}}}

'''traitorously''' (trā′tọr-us-li), ''adv.'' [< ME. ''traiterously, treterously''; < ''traitorous'' + ''-ly''<sup>2</sup>.] In a traitorous manner; in violation of allegiance and trust; treacherously; perfidiously.

{{smaller block|They had ''traitorously'' endeavoured to subvert the fundamental laws.
{{right|''Clarendon.''}}}}

readings.c2s2:
¶
'''traitorousness''' (trā′tọr-us-nes), ''n.'' The quality of being traitorous or treacherous; treachery. ''Bailey'', 1727.

'''traitory†''' (trā′tọr-i), ''n.'' [ME. ''traitorie'', ''traiterye'', < OF. *''traitorie'', < ''traitor'', a traitor: see ''traitor''.] Treachery; betrayal; treason.

{{smaller block|Tho com another companye<br>That had ydon the ''traiterye'',<br>The harm, the grete wikkednesse,<br>That any herte couthe gesse.
{{right|''Chaucer'', House of Fame, l. 1812.}}}}

'''traitress''' (trā′tres), ''n.'' [< F. ''traîtresse''; as ''traitor'' + ''-ess''.] A woman who betrays her trust; a perfidious woman; a female traitor: often used in a weakened, half-playful sense.

{{smaller block|Ah, little ''traitress!'' none must know . . .<br>What vanity full dearly bought,<br>Joined to thine eye's dark witchcraft, drew<br>My spell-bound steps to Benvenue.
{{right|''Scott'', L. of the L., vi. 28.}}}}

'''traject''' (trā-jekt′), ''v. t.'' [< L. ''trajectus'', pp. of ''trajicere'' (LL. also rarely ''transjacere''), throw or cast over, carry over, ship over, transport, also transfix, < ''trans'', through, across, + ''jacere'', throw: see ''jet''<sup>1</sup>.] To throw or cast (across or through). [Rare.]

{{smaller block|Thou knowst that to be Cerberus, and him<br>The ferriman who from the rivers brim<br>''Trajected'' thee.
{{right|''Heywood'', Dialogues (Works, ed. Pearson, 1874, VI. 236).}}}}

{{smaller block|If the sun's light be ''trajected'' through three or more cross prisms successively.
{{right|''Newton'', Opticks, I. i., Exper. 10.}}}}

readings.c2s3:
¶
'''traject''' (traj′ekt), ''n.'' [< OF. ''traject, trajet,'' a ferry, a passage over, = It. ''tragetto, tragitto,'' < L. ''trajectus,'' a passage over, < ''trajicere,'' throw over: see ''traject, v.'' Cf. ''treget.''] 1†. A ferry; a passage or place for passing over water with boats (by some commentators said to mean the boat itself).

{{smaller block|Bring them, I pray thee, with imagin'd speed<br>Vnto the ''tranect'' [read ''traiect,'' i. e. ''traject,'' as in various modern editions], to the common ferry<br>Which trades to Venice.
{{right|''Shak.,'' M. of V., iii. 4. 54 (folio 1623).}}}}

2. A trajectory. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|The ''traject'' of comets.
{{right|''Is. Taylor.'' (''Imp. Dict.'')}}}}

3. The act of throwing across or transporting; transmission; transference. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|At the best, however, this ''traject'' [that of printing from Asia] was but that of the germ of life, which Sir W. Thomson, in a famous discourse, suggested had been carried to this earth from some other sphere by meteoric agency.
{{right|''Athenæum.'' (''Imp. Dict.'')}}}}

'''trajection''' (trā-jek′shọn), ''n.'' [= It. ''trajezione,'' < L. ''trajectio(n-),'' a crossing over, passage, transposition (of words), < ''trajicere,'' throw over, convey over: see ''traject.''] 1. The act of trajecting; a casting or darting through or across; a crossing; a passage.

{{smaller block|My due for thy ''trajection'' downe here lay.
{{right|''Heywood,'' Dialogues (Works, ed. Pearson, 1874, VI. 232).}}}}

{{smaller block|Of this sort might be the spectre at the Rubicon, Cæsar hesitating that ''trajection.''
{{right|''Evelyn,'' True Religion, I. 144.}}}}

2. In ''gram.'' and ''rhet.,'' transposition: same as ''hyperbaton'' (''a''). [Rare.]

readings.c3s2:
¶
'''tralatition†''' (tral-ạ̄-tish′ọn), ''n.'' [Irreg. for ''tralation'' (after ''tralatitious'').] A departure from the literal use of words; a metaphor.

'''tralatitious†''' (tral-ạ̄-tish′us), ''a.'' [=It. ''tralatizio'', < L. ''tralaticius'', ''tralatitius'', equiv. to ''translaticius'', ''translatitius'', < ''translatus'', pp. of ''transferre'', transfer: see ''translate''.] Metaphorical; not literal.

{{smaller block|Unless we could contrive a perfect set of new words, there is no speaking of the Deity without using our old ones in a ''tralatitious'' sense. ''Stackhouse'', Hist. Bible, iv. 1.}}

'''tralatitiously†''' (tral-ạ̄-tish′us-li), ''adv.'' Metaphorically; not in a literal sense.

{{smaller block|Written Language is ''tralatitiously'' so called, because it is made to represent to the Eye the same Letters and Words which are pronounced.
{{right|''Holder'', Elements of Speech, p. 8.}}}}

'''tralineate†''' (trạ̄-lin′ẹ̄-āt), ''v. i.'' [After It. ''tralignare'', degenerate, < L. ''trans'', across, + ''linea'', line: see ''line''<sup>2</sup>.] To deviate in course or direction.

{{smaller block|If you ''tralineate'' from your father's mind,
What are you else but of a bastard-kind?
{{right|''Dryden'', Wife of Bath, l. 396.}}}}

'''Trallian''' (tral′ian), ''a.'' [< L. ''Trallianus'' (< Gr. Τραλλιανός), of Tralles, < ''Tralles'', also ''Trallis'', < Gr. Τράλλεις, also Τράλλις, a city of Lydia.] Of or pertaining to the ancient Greek city of Tralles, in Asia Minor, or its inhabitants.—{{smaller|'''Trallian school''', a school of Greek Hellenistic sculpture of the third century {{sc|b. c.}}, of which the great surviving work is}}
